section: compiler
synopsis: The compiler session always requires a `HomeUnit`.
description: {
    We change `HomeUnitEnv` to always require a `HomeUnit` value.
    Most parts of the compiler simply `panic` when the `HomeUnit` is missing,
    and GHC API users are often forced to create dummy `HomeUnit` values for the API any way.
    Thus, by demanding a `HomeUnit` in the `HomeUnitEnv`, we can avoid many sources of `panic`s, and simplify
    multiple code paths and some APIs.

    We can always create a dummy `HomeUnit` value based on the `UnitId` of the
    `HomeUnitEnv.homeUnitEnv_dflags.homeUnitId_`.
    This `HomeUnit` might be wrong, as we don't know exactly what kind of `HomeUnit` it is until we have called
    `initUnits` on the `HomeUnitEnv` contents themselves.
    Once `initUnits` is called, we store the corrected `HomeUnit` in the `HomeUnitEnv`.

    From an API perspective, very little changes, since when users didn't have a real `HomeUnit` value, they needed to create
    a dummy value already for most GHC API functions.

    The reason the `HomeUnit` used to be optional is to indicate which parts of the GHC API require certain initialisation steps,
    such as calling `initUnits` on the `HomeUnitEnv`. The `HomeUnit` is a poor reflection of this requirement, which is why
    we decided to make it mandatory whenever there is a `HomeUnitEnv`.
    In the future, we might want to reflect this information based on the existence of a `HomeUnitEnv`, which we should only create
    if the compiler session actually needs it.
}
